{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1522782-new-tsalagi-cherokee-pantheon-or-just-more-manitou-for-scion","id":1522782,"title":"New Tsalagi (Cherokee) pantheon, or just more Manitou for Scion","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-09-19T02:32:44-07:00","id":1522782,"title":"New Tsalagi (Cherokee) pantheon, or just more Manitou for Scion","content":"I am a Cherokee and have studied our religion for quite a while. I made a pantheon using this, although there are a lot of similarities to the Manitou. Anyone has my official permission to use this in their Scion games.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> The letter &quot;v&quot; in Tsalagi is pronounced &quot;uh&quot; and the TS is kind of like a &quot;ch&quot; sound. The letter &quot;u&quot; is always an &quot;oo&quot; sound. The letter &quot;a&quot; is pronounced &quot;ah&quot; and the letter &quot;i&quot; is pronounced &quot;ee&quot; while the letter &quot;e&quot; is pronounced &quot;eh&quot;.<br /> <br /> Asgina is the tsalagi word for &quot;spirit&quot;, similar to manitou for the Anishinaabek. The Nunnehi are powerful spirits similar to demigods and are above most other spirits so it made sense to me to call the pantheon this.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> ASGINA NUNNEHI<br /> <br /> <br /> Like the manitou but often gender-flipped versions of them.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Primordial: Unetlanvhi \u2013 the Sun Goddess, the Apportioner<br /> <br /> Callings: Creator, Sage, Judge<br /> <br /> The Sun who created everything, kind of a cross between the Kami Amaterasu and if Kitchi-Manitou was gender flipped.<br /> <br /> Purviews: Sun, Fortune, Stars, Prosperity, Fertility, Order<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Eduda Suli - Grandfather Buzzard<br /> <br /> Callings: Guardian, Sage, Judge<br /> <br /> Geezhigo-Quae of the manitou gender flipped and associated with vultures, hawks, and other large raptors. Dried the world after D\u00e2yuni's\u00ef brought the land up, but inadvertently formed mountains, hills, and valleys by doing this.<br /> <br /> Purviews: Beasts (all), Sky, Order, Wild, Epic Stamina<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> D\u00e2yuni's\u00ef- the Water Beetle<br /> <br /> Callings: Healer, Sage, Hunter<br /> <br /> Uzzu-Kumik-Quae gender flipped, brought the mud up from the waters to form the land.<br /> <br /> Purviews: Earth, Water, Journeys, Wild, Beasts (Aquatic Beetles and Turtles)<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Kanati \u2013 the Lucky Hunter<br /> <br /> Callings: Trickster, Leader, Hunter<br /> <br /> The First Man. Like Maudjee-Kawiss but far less uptight. A bit of a trickster as well. Kept all the animals in a cave so he could hunt them one at a time until his sons discovered the cave and accidently let them all out.<br /> <br /> Purviews: Epic Dexterity, Fortune, Sky, Deception, Prosperity<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Selu \u2013 the Corn Mother<br /> <br /> Callings: Guardian, Lover, Healer<br /> <br /> The First Woman, very similar to Winona of the Manitou but also the one who created corn and beans. Killed by her children the thunder twins when they mistook her for a raven mocker posing as her, but they later brought her back by following a ritual she gave them.<br /> <br /> Purviews: Earth, Fertility, Prosperity, Health, Death<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Jistu \u2013 Rabbit<br /> <br /> Callings: Trickster, Hunter, Warrior<br /> <br /> Basically Nana\u2019b\u2019oozoo but more closely associated with rabbits. A trickster and culture hero.<br /> <br /> Purviews: Beasts (rabbits and hares only), Chaos, Journeys, Deception, Artistry (Dance, Acting, Storytelling)<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> The Thunder Twins<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Asgaya Gigagei \u2013 the Red Man/Red Woman, Kapoonis, Lightning<br /> <br /> Callings: Warrior, Sage, Creator<br /> <br /> Learned medicine, magic, and how to create fire spears (aka lightning). Went on many journeys to find herbs (he was the one who discovered tobacco) or knowledge. Could be male or female depending on what he thought was best for the situation (when healing he would often take on the gender of the one being healed to put them at ease). The smartest of the thunder twins.<br /> <br /> Purviews: Fire, Journeys, Sky, Healing, Forge<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Inage Utasuhi \u2013 the Wild One, Enumclaw, Thunder<br /> <br /> Callings: Warrior, Hunter, Liminal<br /> <br /> Trained until he could hurl massive boulders with ease, often gets his brother into trouble. Enjoys living in the woods and remote places. The strongest of the thunder twins.<br /> <br /> Purviews: Epic Strength, Epic Stamina, Wild, Sky, Chaos<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Titans: Asgina Uya<br /> <br /> <br /> Tsul \u2018Kalu \u2013 the sloping eyed giant<br /> <br /> Rapacity, Dominance<br /> <br /> He is of giant proportions, and dwells in a great mountain of the Blue Ridge Range, in North-western Virginia. He appears to have possessed all the game in the district as his private property. Not that bad really but likes to be left alone. Able to turn himself invisible and is a very skilled hunter.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Ocasta \u2013 the Stonecoat<br /> <br /> Fecundity, Dominance<br /> <br /> A giant made of stone. Created the raven mockers, was captured, then as he was dying had a change of heart and taught the people medicine songs and magic. Could never decide if he wanted to be good or evil. His spirit might make a good guide.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> U\u2019tlun\u2019ta\u2019 \u2013 the Spearfinger<br /> <br /> Submission, Dominance<br /> <br /> The first of the Raven mockers, evil sorcerers who can shapeshift into owls and eat livers to stay young. This one is the most evil Titan of the pantheon.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> K\u00e2'lan\u00fb Ahkyeli'sk\u00ef - Raven Mockers, evil sorcerers who can shapeshift into owls and eat livers to stay young. Often are fallen medicine men. Otherwise similar to the Wendigo.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Overworld:<br /> <br /> Galun\u2019lati - The sky vault, basically Skyworld.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Underworld:<br /> <br /> Ulasigi \u2013 Similar to The World but the seasons are backwards (winter is hot, summer is cold, etc.).<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Creatures:<br /> <br /> <br /> Tlanuwa \u2013 Basically like Manitou thunderbirds but with metal or obsidian feathers instead of lightning powers<br /> <br /> Uktena \u2013 Basically Manitou water panthers. No real difference<br /> <br /> Junsdi \u2013 Basically Bagwajiwinnini/Pukwudgie, little people with very long hair<br /> <br /> The Asgina Nunnehi also have supernatural animals like the Manitou.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Signature Purview would be the same as the Manitou.<br /> <br /> Pride and Dream seem like a good fit for virtues, but they also have a large emphasis on balance similar to the Shen.<br /> <br /> All in all, other than individual goddesses and gods being a little different, they are extremely similar to the Manitou, and would likely get lumped in together with them by other gods.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Religion: Kituwah<br /> <br /> VERY similar to Midewiwin, except there is both an emphasis on balance and &quot;becoming Yvwi&quot;, or a &quot;true human being&quot;. This part is very similar to Hinduism. The afterlife is temporary, everyone gets reincarnated eventually. There are no &quot;castes&quot; though, where you are on the path to becoming &quot;Yvwi&quot; is between you and the spirits. The point is to keep trying. There are clans though, but no clan is superior to another. This factors into the symbol of the Cherokee and the Asgina Nunnehi, which is a seven pointed star (which represents the seven Tsalagi clans). Fire is also a big sacred element in being Kituwah, and the chief ritual is the Stomp Dance, in which practitioners dance around a fire singing medicine songs all night (yes, the ENTIRE night) one day per month after purifying themselves through washing in the river.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Feedback is welcome!<br /> <br /> I already posted this on reddit and thought I should probably do that here too.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28198,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28198-ahisgewaya","user":"Ahisgewaya","date":". 09-19-2023, 05:52 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":9}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28198-ahisgewaya","name":"Ahisgewaya","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28198_2.jpg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-19T06:45:38-07:00","id":1522790,"title":"","content":"Oh,cool stuff!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4621-nicolas-milioni","name":"Nicolas Milioni","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4621_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3605"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-19T16:48:28-07:00","id":1522807,"title":"","content":"Thank you for sharing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25049-zakrulz","name":"ZakRulz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25049_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"238"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-19T19:28:26-07:00","id":1522812,"title":"","content":"&quot;Nunnehi&quot;<br /> That word always confused me.<br /> Is it a general name, or is it the name of a specific species?<br /> I read about them on Wikipedia, it was so specific that I felt that calling the pantheon that would be like calling the Arab pantheon &quot;djinn&quot; or the Aztec pantheon &quot;nahual&quot;\u200b<br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/N%C3%BB%C3%B1n%C3%AB%27h%C3%AF\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/static/images/icons/enwiki-25.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/N%C3%BB%C3%B1n%C3%AB%27h%C3%AF\" target=\"_blank\"> N&ucirc;&ntilde;n&euml;'h&iuml; - Wikipedia </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/N%C3%BB%C3%B1n%C3%AB%27h%C3%AF\" target=\"_blank\"> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/N%C3%BB%C3%B1n%C3%AB%27h%C3%AF </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28200-plebsparks","name":"PlebSparks","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28200_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"22"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-20T14:57:59-07:00","id":1522858,"title":"","content":"Thanks for this! Are there good sources an outsider could read to learn more about traditional Cherokee religion, cosmology, mythology, etc.?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26114-muskrat","name":"Muskrat","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"75"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-21T18:46:24-07:00","id":1522932,"title":"","content":"There are some problems with that wikipedia article. It mentions the Junsdi and the Nunnehi as the same for one thing, which they most certainly are not. Junsdi, as I have noted in the original post, are a lot like the Bagwajiwinnini/Pukwudgie. They look like tiny humans and have long hair. They also play good natured pranks on people, which the Nunnehi do not do. I would say the Junsdi are more like the &quot;fairies&quot; of Europe than Nunnehi. Nunnehi were said to live in specific mountains and dirt mounds (often ones that had been used in funerary practices in the past), much like the Theoi live on Mount Olympus, not like a &quot;hidden fairy village&quot; which the article implies. They also are referred to as &quot;the ancestors&quot; in many stories, which is also unlike fairies. Finally, human beings sometimes would be taken away by the Nunnehi to become one of them. This could be seen as similar to stories of Changelings, but I don't think it is since the human in question was always an adult, not a child or newborn, and only has to stay with the Nunnehi if they eat their food. This makes it more like gods taking a human to become a god with them (Hades and Persephone comes to mind).\u200b<br /> <br /> Nunnehi are kind of hard to place, as humans could become Nunnehi but they also are ancient godlike beings. The most prominent myth about them was when they came out of a mound to protect a group of Tsalagi. This seems to me what they are going for in Scion with &quot;gods&quot;. They don't really fit anywhere else. They aren't like Djinn as all of them are benevolent (or at least on the side of humanity, and some stories imply them to be actual ancestor spirits). Asgina literally means &quot;spirit&quot;, which is why it really fits with the word &quot;manitou&quot;. A nunnehi is basically an immortal, benevolent, superior spirit. This made it make sense to call the pantheon this, as the actual pantheon doesn't really have a name other than as &quot;the first people&quot;, much like the Manitou. Likewise, Kanati is the leader of the Cherokee family of gods (himself, Selu, and their children) but is also the first man to have ever lived. This is similar to how Scion treats Winona within the Manitou, even though she is also the first human woman.<br /> <br /> A lot of Native American mythology is like this. Gods are not something &quot;outside&quot; of humanity, but something we come from and to aspire to. This also fits with the Cherokee belief of &quot;becoming Yuwi&quot;, or becoming the true human being (a human god in other words). This is why I said there are many similarities with how I understand Hinduism works. Are the Nunnehi just spirits that have &quot;become Yuwi&quot; and therefore a better name for the pantheon would be Yuwi? I don't know, but I don't think so since some of the members of the pantheon have flaws (Inage Utasuhi convincing his brother that their mother is actually a raven mocker pretending to be their mother is a stellar example of these flaws). Someone who has become a true human being would not have done this, so at that time Inage Utasuhi had not &quot;become Yuwi&quot;. This is why I didn't choose &quot;Asgina Yuwi&quot; as the pantheon name.<br /> <br /> I am a practicing Kituwah (which means Nighthawk, referring to how we dance all night around a fire like the Nighthawk is a nocturnal bird). Most of my information comes from talking to elders at the stomp dance and reading the &quot;Swimmer Manuscript&quot;, a journal written by a Cherokee self professed medicine man, and various Cherokee authors who likely used the Swimmer Manuscript as well. I would also recommend &quot;Walking on the Wind&quot; by Michael Tlanusta Garrett which you can find on Amazon. He uses a lot of the Swimmer Manuscript which might be difficult to find nowadays (I bought my copy at a rare book store around 20 years ago). The swimmer manuscript itself has large sections written in Tsalagi, so that also might cause some problems if you don't know our language. Garrett's book is entirely in English however so I would recommend it as a good starting place if you want to learn more.\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28198,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28198-ahisgewaya","user":"Ahisgewaya","date":". 09-21-2023, 09:49 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28198-ahisgewaya","name":"Ahisgewaya","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28198_2.jpg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-21T19:01:56-07:00","id":1522934,"title":"","content":"My first two replies were double posts and didn't say everything I wanted to on this, so please delete the previous two as well as this one (leaving only the third, in other words #8 is the one I want to stay) if you are a moderator. I don't know how to delete a reply of mine on here yet.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28198,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28198-ahisgewaya","user":"Ahisgewaya","date":". 09-21-2023, 10:04 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28198-ahisgewaya","name":"Ahisgewaya","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28198_2.jpg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-30T20:55:08-07:00","id":1523388,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PlebSparks</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1522782-new-tsalagi-cherokee-pantheon-or-just-more-manitou-for-scion?p=1522812#post1522812\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">&quot;Nunnehi&quot;<br /> That word always confused me.<br /> Is it a general name, or is it the name of a specific species?<br /> I read about them on Wikipedia, it was so specific that I felt that calling the pantheon that would be like calling the Arab pantheon &quot;djinn&quot; or the Aztec pantheon &quot;nahual&quot;\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200b<br /> <br /> <br /> All right, I'm going to try this one more time and see if it works this time.<br /> <br /> There are some problems with that wikipedia article. It mentions the Junsdi and the Nunnehi as the same for one thing, which they most certainly are not. Junsdi, as I have noted in the original post, are a lot like the Bagwajiwinnini/Pukwudgie. They look like tiny humans and have long hair. They also play good natured pranks on people, which the Nunnehi do not do. I would say the Junsdi are more like the &quot;fairies&quot; of Europe than Nunnehi. Nunnehi were said to live in specific mountains and dirt mounds (often ones that had been used in funerary practices in the past), much like the Theoi live on Mount Olympus, not like a &quot;hidden fairy village&quot; which the article implies. They also are referred to as &quot;the ancestors&quot; in many stories, which is also unlike fairies. Finally, human beings sometimes would be taken away by the Nunnehi to become one of them. This could be seen as similar to stories of Changelings, but I don't think it is since the human in question was always an adult, not a child or newborn, and only has to stay with the Nunnehi if they eat their food. This makes it more like gods taking a human to become a god with them (Hades and Persephone comes to mind).\u200b<br /> <br /> Nunnehi are kind of hard to place, as humans could become Nunnehi but they also are ancient godlike beings. The most prominent myth about them was when they came out of a mound to protect a group of Tsalagi. This seems to me what they are going for in Scion with &quot;gods&quot;. They don't really fit anywhere else. They aren't like Djinn as all of them are benevolent (or at least on the side of humanity, and some stories imply them to be actual ancestor spirits). Asgina literally means &quot;spirit&quot;, which is why it really fits with the word &quot;manitou&quot;. A nunnehi is basically an immortal, benevolent, superior spirit. This made it make sense to call the pantheon this, as the actual pantheon doesn't really have a name other than as &quot;the first people&quot;, much like the Manitou. Likewise, Kanati is the leader of the Cherokee family of gods (himself, Selu, and their children) but is also the first man to have ever lived. This is similar to how Scion treats Winona within the Manitou, even though she is also the first human woman.<br /> <br /> A lot of Native American mythology is like this. Gods are not something &quot;outside&quot; of humanity, but something we come from and to aspire to. This also fits with the Cherokee belief of &quot;becoming Yuwi&quot;, or becoming the true human being (a human god in other words). This is why I said there are many similarities with how I understand Hinduism works. Are the Nunnehi just spirits that have &quot;become Yuwi&quot; and therefore a better name for the pantheon would be Yuwi? I don't know, but I don't think so since some of the members of the pantheon have flaws (Inage Utasuhi convincing his brother that their mother is actually a raven mocker pretending to be their mother is a stellar example of these flaws). Someone who has become a true human being would not have done this, so at that time Inage Utasuhi had not &quot;become Yuwi&quot;. This is why I didn't choose &quot;Asgina Yuwi&quot; as the pantheon name.\u200b<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Muskrat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1522782-new-tsalagi-cherokee-pantheon-or-just-more-manitou-for-scion?p=1522858#post1522858\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Thanks for this! Are there good sources an outsider could read to learn more about traditional Cherokee religion, cosmology, mythology, etc.?</div> </div> </div> </div>I am a practicing Kituwah (which means Nighthawk, referring to how we dance all night around a fire like the Nighthawk is a nocturnal bird). Most of my information comes from talking to elders at the stomp dance and reading the &quot;Swimmer Manuscript&quot;, a journal written by a Cherokee self professed medicine man, and various Cherokee authors who likely used the Swimmer Manuscript as well. I would also recommend &quot;Walking on the Wind&quot; by Michael Tlanusta Garrett which you can find on Amazon. He uses a lot of the Swimmer Manuscript which might be difficult to find nowadays (I bought my copy at a rare book store around 20 years ago). The swimmer manuscript itself has large sections written in Tsalagi, so that also might cause some problems if you don't know our language. Garrett's book is entirely in English however so I would recommend it as a good starting place if you want to learn more.\u200b\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28198-ahisgewaya","name":"Ahisgewaya","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28198_2.jpg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-01T21:48:33-07:00","id":1523452,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ahisgewaya</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1522782-new-tsalagi-cherokee-pantheon-or-just-more-manitou-for-scion?p=1523388#post1523388\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u200bI am a practicing Kituwah (which means Nighthawk, referring to how we dance all night around a fire like the Nighthawk is a nocturnal bird). Most of my information comes from talking to elders at the stomp dance and reading the &quot;Swimmer Manuscript&quot;, a journal written by a Cherokee self professed medicine man, and various Cherokee authors who likely used the Swimmer Manuscript as well. I would also recommend &quot;Walking on the Wind&quot; by Michael Tlanusta Garrett which you can find on Amazon. He uses a lot of the Swimmer Manuscript which might be difficult to find nowadays (I bought my copy at a rare book store around 20 years ago). The swimmer manuscript itself has large sections written in Tsalagi, so that also might cause some problems if you don't know our language. Garrett's book is entirely in English however so I would recommend it as a good starting place if you want to learn more.\u200b\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks!<br /> <br /> Some of how you describe the Nunnehi sounds a bit like the Tuatha de Danaan of the Irish. I think people often take the Greco-Roman deities as their reference point for how deities in polytheistic pantheons are, but various people have conceived of their deities quite differently. Even the Greeks themselves differed in how they depicted the gods--Homer represents them as being petty and vengeful, while Sophocles represents them as transcendent, all-knowing, perfectly just entities.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26114-muskrat","name":"Muskrat","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"75"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}